#
# prima-materia — the case series against the elixir, and the standing of the
# man who assembled it
#
# The protocol here is not the compiler's. It was carried out in the year 823
# by Han Yu, in the epitaph he wrote for his kinsman by marriage, and it is
# entered as a protocol because it does what the class requires: it states an
# inclusion rule, applies it, and reports the outcomes against a claim this
# graph holds. No modern evidence is cited and none is needed for what it
# shows.
#

pmc:YiyaoBaizhe a pm:Testing ;
    rdfs:label "The seven men ruined by the drug"@en, "Die sieben Männer, die das Mittel zugrunde richtete"@de ;
    skos:definition "A case series assembled in 823 against the claim that mineral elixirs confer deathlessness, and stated as such. The author writes that he does not know from what age the doctrine of taking these things arose, that the men it has killed cannot be counted, and that the world's longing for it only grows. He then states his inclusion rule: what is recorded in documents and what is heard at second hand he passes over, and takes only those he saw with his own eyes and personally associated with and who were ruined by the drug, six or seven gentlemen, to serve as a warning to the age. He names seven, all of them men of rank whom the world knows. The Erudite for whom the epitaph is written met a fangshi while serving in the southeast, received the drug method, took it, passed blood repeatedly, and after four years, the illness growing acute, died at forty-eight; the method is given as a cauldron filled with lead, hollowed in the middle and filled with mercury, sealed at the four edges and fired to make cinnabar. Of the seven: the Minister of Works, having eaten mercury, fell ill and described it himself as a burning iron staff driven from the crown down through him, breaking into fire and shooting out at the apertures and joints, so that he howled with mad pain and begged to be let die; mercury was found on his bedding, and when it broke out the pain stopped; he spat blood for more than ten years and so perished. The Palace Censor died of an abscess that broke out on his back. The Minister of Justice, dying, said to the author: I was misled by the drug. That man's younger brother, a Vice-Minister, died in a single morning with no illness. The commissioner of Xiangyang met the author by boat, sent the others away, said that he had obtained a secret drug and could not alone escape death, and left him a vessel of it to be taken as pills with jujube flesh; a year later he was ill, and someone from his household reported that the drug he had taken before was the wrong one, that he was about to purge it, and that when purged he would be well; he was ill two years and died. The Censor-in-chief of Dongchuan, dying, passed blood and flesh with his urine, in pain past bearing, and begged for death. The General of the Golden Guard ate the same fangshi's drug and died in exile by the sea."@en ;
    skos:note "The inclusion rule is the reason this is a protocol and not a polemic. It excludes exactly the two classes of report that make a case series worthless: what stands written elsewhere, and what the author was told. Nothing in this collection, ancient or modern, states a cleaner criterion, and it is stated in the ninth century by a man writing a funeral inscription."@en ;
    skos:note "What the series can and cannot settle. It reports seven men who took mineral preparations and died, four of them with a course of illness that the report ties to the taking, and two with symptoms that a modern reader will recognise without the graph needing to say so. It does not establish a rate, because the denominator is missing: how many took such preparations and did not die of them is not asked, and the author's own selection is of the ruined. What it does establish is that the outcome the transmission excludes did occur, repeatedly, in men the author knew."@en ;
    skos:note "The edition's commentary carries the case that would have counted most and that the epitaph itself leaves unsaid. The fangshi who supplied the drug was recommended to the reigning emperor through a favourite; he prepared the cinnabar preparation; the emperor took it and, in the commentary's phrase, suddenly abandoned the myriad states; on the next reign the fangshi was handed to the metropolitan prefecture and put to death, and the man who had recommended him was demoted and died of the drug in his place of exile. That the ruling house both took the preparation and executed its maker is the sharpest thing in the apparatus, and it is in the apparatus and not in the text."@en ;
    skos:note "The author says six or seven and names seven. The approximation is left as he wrote it rather than corrected to the count, because a series whose author was not sure of his own number is reporting from memory and not from a list, which is a fact about the series."@en ;
    pm:tests pmc:JindanYieldsCapacities ;
    pm:examinationState pm:casesWithDeviation ;
    pm:falsifiedBy "Men who take the mineral preparations live at or beyond the ordinary span and die of causes unconnected with the taking, with no course of illness following it." ;
    pm:baseRate "The span and manner of death of men of the same rank and period who did not take mineral preparations, which this series does not supply." ;
    pm:counterSearch pm:counterSearchFoundOpposition ;
    pm:counterSearchNote "Carried on 2026-09-10 over the two corpora this file rests on and recorded as a node at pmc:FushiSuccessSearch. Two things came back. In the transmission itself, one success is claimed: the fourth chapter of the Baopuzi names a scholar of the end of the Han said to have become an immortal by the Great Clarity elixir and to have listed more than forty others, which this graph holds at pmc:YinjunDexian and which is second-hand by its own account. In the witness's own collected works, a poem to a friend says that since their parting he knows the friend has had the golden elixir transmitted to him, and begs a knife-point of it to save his sick body; the edition's commentators argue over whether that shows the author of this series took mineral drugs himself. Both findings weaken the series, the first by supplying a claimed positive case and the second by unsettling the witness, and both are recorded rather than set aside."@en ;
    pm:examinedBy "Han Yu, in the epitaph for the late Erudite of the Grand Academy Li, written in 823; the protocol is his and not the compiler's" ;
    pm:protocolUpdated "2026-09-10" ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Waidan ;
    dcterms:source "Han Yu, epitaph for the late Erudite of the Grand Academy Li, in Wubaijia zhu Changli wenji, Wenyuange Siku quanshu text, juan 34" .

pmc:HanyuFushiDispute a pm:Disputing ;
    rdfs:label "Whether the man who warned the age took the drugs himself"@en, "Ob der Mann, der das Zeitalter warnte, die Mittel selbst nahm"@de ;
    skos:definition "The commentators of the edition read here argue the question in two places and reach opposite answers. On one side: a poem of the author's to a friend, sent while both were old, ends with the lines that since their parting he knows the friend has had the golden elixir transmitted to him, and that he begs a knife-point of it to save this sick body; the commentary adds that the friend was fond of the golden elixir and its ingestion, and that a letter to him on ingesting drugs for long life stands in the collected works of a contemporary. To this some add a line of Bai Juyi's poem on thinking of old friends, that the author took sulphur and never recovered from the illness. On the other side, a commentator answers that the author died in the fourth year of Changqing and that in the third he wrote this very epitaph, enumerating six or seven men ruined by drugs as a warning to the age; and asks how one can pass that over and merely say what the others say."@en ;
    skos:note "The dispute is entered because it bears on the standing of the only case series this collection holds against the elixir, and not because the private conduct of a ninth-century official is of interest. A witness who assembled seven deaths and then asked a friend for a dose of the same class of preparation is not thereby wrong about the seven; he is a witness whose own practice does not follow from his finding, and a record that hid that would be reporting on the strength of its evidence rather than on the evidence."@en ;
    skos:note "The poem does not say that he took anything. It says that he asked. The strongest reading the text supports is a request made in a poem to a friend, and the further step to ingestion is taken by the commentators from Bai Juyi's line and not from the collection itself."@en ;
    skos:note "What the answering commentator offers is not evidence but a consideration of character: that a man who wrote the warning would not have done the thing. That is an argument of the same kind as the one it answers, and neither side of this dispute rests on a witness to the act."@en ;
    pm:disputedClaim pmc:YiyaoBaizhe ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Waidan ;
    dcterms:source "Han Yu, a further poem sent to the supernumerary of Suizhou, with the commentary printed with it, in Wubaijia zhu Changli wenji, Wenyuange Siku quanshu text, juan 10", "Han Yu, epitaph for the late Erudite of the Grand Academy Li, in Wubaijia zhu Changli wenji, Wenyuange Siku quanshu text, juan 34" .
